"""Clean-source American dialogue decisions,with individual contextual retentions."""
REVISIONS={
13:("Here, take this. Use it however you like!",'Give the abrupt handoff and permission a natural spoken rhythm. Here,take this renders the immediate unsolicited offer;however you like conveys 好きに使ってくれ without the older slightly formal use it as you like. Do not name the object before Lil identifies it or invent legitimate ownership,a request,payment or a crime.'),
35:("W-what was that?!",'Keep the existing natural exasperated question and restore the startled windup in な、何なのよ after the unexplained handoff and two shouted pursuit beats. This is bewilderment,not recognition of the sailor or an accusation.'),
43:("Someone just forced this on me...",'Use the American idiom forced this on me for 無理矢理渡されちゃった,conveying an unwanted unsolicited handoff and Lil\'s bemusement. The older shoved this at me can suggest an object thrown toward her. Do not invent an assault,injury,exact carrying method or her agreement to the gift.'),
51:("A book... I think?",'Preserve the pause and tentative self-assessment in 本…かな before Charles examines it. The older bare A book? loses some of Lil\'s puzzled hesitation. Do not put the title or certainty into her line.'),
55:('Let\'s see... "Glassmaking Guide"...','Restore どれどれ,the moment Charles starts examining the object,before he reads its explicit title. Clean speech and clean item14 both name ガラスの製法辞典;the established Glassmaking Guide accurately identifies the technical reference and matches the observed card. Do not introduce a competing literal title,new contents,rarity or effects. Keep the interested trailing pause.'),
65:("If someone's giving you something, you might as well take it.",'Express Charles\'s general pragmatic view in くれるってもんはもらっておけばいい as casual advice. The old Take what you\'re given sounds like a command to accept one\'s lot and weakens the conditional logic behind his self-interested advice. Preserve the idea of something freely offered without guaranteeing ownership or telling Lil to steal.')}
RETAIN_NOTES={
5:'Hey,you. is a natural abrupt American way to get a stranger\'s attention and matches ちょっと、あんた. Keep anonymity and the unexpected interruption;do not turn it into an established acquaintance or add a threat.',
9:'Hm? naturally conveys Lil\'s brief questioning response to being addressed. It neither accepts the gift nor explains the situation;keep the short beat before the unsolicited offer.',
16:'Huh? is natural surprised dialogue for え？ after the handoff offer. Keep this separate reaction before the Guildmaster interrupts rather than merging it with Lil\'s later doubled question.',
20:'There you are! Stop! naturally conveys spotting the fleeing sailor and the rough shouted order in あっ！いやがった！待ちやがれ！. Its terse imperative carries urgency without a fabricated insult,police role,crime or rank. The next Lil reaction and repeated pursuit command preserve the complete commotion.',
23:'H-huh? preserves Lil\'s doubled/startled question as confusion escalates. It is natural spoken hesitation and remains distinct from the first Huh? and later exasperated question.',
27:'See you! is the natural casual farewell for じゃあな！,funny here because the sailor abruptly leaves while Lil is baffled and the Guildmaster pursues him. It adds no destination,promise to return or guaranteed escape.',
31:'Stop! is the natural urgent repeated command for 待ちやがれ！. Its separate speech object preserves the pursuit rhythm and rough intent without adding threats or moving it to Lil.',
39:'What\'s going on,Admiral? naturally asks about the confusing situation while retaining the explicit 提督 address. This is Charles Rochefort18,not Carlo19 or Camille9. Preserve curiosity and the established command relationship without new rank or kinship.',
47:'What is it? is a natural direct question for 何なんです？ after Lil says an object was forced on her. Keep the object unidentified until Lil\'s tentative answer and Charles\'s examination.',
58:'Hm. Might as well keep it. is natural casual advice for ふーん／もらっておけばいいじゃないですか. The preceding handoff supplies the free-offer context. It neither guarantees legal ownership nor adds a game effect or a purchase.',
61:'Huh? But... is a natural unfinished objection and preserves both the questioning reaction and hesitation in え？でも…. Do not finish the objection with an invented theft accusation,ownership claim or plan to return the book.',
68:'Oh,let me see it later. naturally conveys そうだ、後で見せてくださいね as a casual request added after his advice. Later remains prospective;let me carries the request without a new demand for ownership or a guaranteed lending arrangement. The following joke supplies the motive.',
72:'So that\'s your angle! is an idiomatic American way for Lil to recognize the self-interest revealed by Charles asking to see the book. The light,dry accusation preserves …それが目的だったのね and its comic payoff without inventing romance,a criminal scheme or a different motive.'}
